 And you can see here we have 1536 dimensions and we have taken the Amazon Titan Analytics.

7
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:37,440
 So now, how do we select the right embedding model?

8
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:43,680
 So the first thing you need to consider is whether your input text is only text or

9
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:49,400
 is it a combination of text, image and so on, because a lot of models only support text,

10
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:52,640
 some other support text and images as well.

11
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:54,480
 So let's take a look at couple of them.

12
00:00:54,480 --> 00:01:01,520
 So here you have Amazon Titan text embedding V2 model, which supports only text embeddings.

13
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:08,680
 Then we have another model from Amazon Titan multimodal embeddings G1 and its input modalities

14
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:10,640
 are text and image.

15
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:14,880
 So first, you need to consider for your use case, what is the input modality?

16
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:16,680
 The next is the output.

17
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:20,800
 So there are various dimensions in which you can generate the output vector size.

18
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:28,320
 It can be 256, 512, or 1024 and if you can see here for Amazon Titan text embeddings,

19
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:33,520
 it comes in three variations, 512, 256, and 1024.

20
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:39,440
 So for your use case, you also need to consider what is the max input text token size

21
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:43,880
 or the text character size and based on that, you can select the embedding model.

22
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:49,920
 So you can see for the Titan text embedding model, max input text token 8192 and max input

23
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:54,440
 text characters is 50,000.

24
00:01:54,440 --> 00:01:57,880
 So how do you select the right embedding model for your use case?

25
00:01:57,880 --> 00:02:01,720
 So there's a trade-off between quality, cost, and latency.

26
00:02:01,720 --> 00:02:07,840
 So if you select a higher dimensional embedding and if you see here, we have 1024, 512,

27
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:08,840
 and 256.

28
00:02:08,840 --> 00:02:13,320
 So if you select a higher one, maybe 1024, you have better recall, but there is going to

29
00:02:13,320 --> 00:02:15,880
 be higher cost.

30
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:19,680
 And obviously if you select a lower dimensional embedding, it's going to be faster, cheaper,

31
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:22,040
 but there's going to be lower accuracy.

32
00:02:22,040 --> 00:02:27,000
 Then you should also match the language and multilingual needs that you might have for

33
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:28,160
 a use case.

34
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:33,200
 So each embedding model supports different languages, so in this case, for Amazon Titan

35
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:39,040
 text, you can see it supports English and 100 plus languages, but it's still in the preview

36
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:40,040
 mode.

37
00:02:40,040 --> 00:02:44,160
 So you should review the multilingual needs for your use case.

38
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:48,800
 And then you should also consider your inference type, whether you want an on-demand or

39
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:49,960
 provision throughput.

